7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
other requests get handled. The Solution: Don't Wait Node.js solves this by not waiting for slow operations. Instead of stopping, Node.js says: "Go do that

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
slow thing. I'll check back later. Meanwhile, I'll run other code." But who does the "checking back later"? That's the Event Loop. What is the

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Event Loop? The Event Loop is a continuous process that checks if there's any work to do and executes it. Think of it as a

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
supervisor who constantly asks: "Is anything ready to run?" The Three Essential Parts To understand the event loop, you need to know three things: 1.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Call Stack (What's Running Now) The call stack is a list of functions currently executing. 2. Task Queue (What's Waiting to Run) The task queue

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
is a waiting area for callbacks that are ready to execute. 3. Event Loop (The Bridge) The event loop constantly checks: Is the call stack

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
empty? If yes, take the first task from the queue and run it. How the Event Loop Works A Simple Example Let's trace through this

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
code: console.log('1. Start'); setTimeout(() => { console.log('2. Timer finished'); }, 0); console.log('3. End'); Step-by-step execution: STEP 1: console.log('1. Start') Call Stack: \[console.log\] Output: "1. Start"

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Call Stack: \[\] (empty) STEP 2: setTimeout(...) Call Stack: \[setTimeout\] * setTimeout says: "I'll call this callback after 0ms" * The timer is handed off

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
to the system Call Stack: \[\] (empty) STEP 3: console.log('3. End') Call Stack: \[console.log\] Output: "3. End" Call Stack: \[\] (empty) STEP 4: Timer completes

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
(0ms later) * The callback moves to the Task Queue Task Queue: \[timerCallback\] STEP 5: Event Loop sees empty call stack * Takes callback from

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
queue Call Stack: \[timerCallback\] Output: "2. Timer finished" Call Stack: \[\] (empty) Why Node.js Needs an Event Loop Without an Event Loop (Blocking) User 1:

