async (req, res) => { const data = await fs.promises.readFile('file.txt'); res.json({ data }); }); // During file read, other requests are handled normally Summary: The

46
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Event Loop in One Paragraph The event loop is a continuous process that: Checks if the call stack is empty Takes the next callback from

47
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:50,000
the task queue Executes it Repeats forever This allows Node.js to handle thousands of concurrent connections with a single thread by never waiting. When an

48
00:07:50,000 --> 00:08:00,000
operation would block (like reading a file), Node.js hands it off to the system, continues running other code, and the event loop brings the callback

49
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:10,000
back when it's ready. The Bottom Line The event loop is a task manager. It hands out tasks and processes completions. Node.js needs it because

50
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:20,000
it's single-threaded. Without the event loop, every slow operation would freeze the entire server. The event loop enables scalability. By never waiting, Node.js can handle

51
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:30,000
thousands of concurrent connections with minimal resources. Your job: Write non-blocking code. Use async versions of functions. Offload CPU work. Let the event loop do

52
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:40,000
its job. The event loop is the secret sauce of Node.js. Respect it. Understand it. And your applications will fly. May 9, 2026May 8, 2026May

